link command
-- creating links between two files (acts as shortcut to a file)
two types
1)soft link- if you make any changes in the actual file, it will get reflected in the softlink.
if you delete actual file, softlink will not work. here actual file and softlink file have different inode numbers
here old file and new file have different permissions (we cannot chmod on new file and it will be as it is)
if we rename the actual file soft link wont work
ex: ln -s temp1/temp2/temp3/file1 link1 ==> the shortcut of path will be saved in link1 as a file
(A hard link is a file all its own, and the file references or points to the exact spot on a hard drive where the
Inode stores the data. A soft link isn't a separate file, it points to the name of the original file, rather than to
a spot on the hard drive)
Inode : store information about files and directories (folders), such as file ownership, access mode (read,
write, execute permissions), and file type

It indicates memory address for particular file
2)hard link- if you make any changes in the actual file, it will get reflected in the hardlink
if you delete actual file, hardlink will work. here actualfile and hardlink file have same inode numbers
here old file and new file file have same permissions .
even if we rename or delete the original file inode number of hardlink file wont be changed
ex: ln temp1/temp2/temp3/file1 link2 ==> the shortcut of path will be saved in link2 as a file
ls -i hardlink ==> it will display inode number (here both actual file and hardlink file have same inode
number)

